## Better fix than disabling patching

My earlier recommendation was to disable the timers, but that has a compliance cost. A better approach keeps automatic patching:

**Patch the image during the build, and leave the timers on.** The base image is old, and the Packer log showed 26 upgradable packages at build time. A new server then boots with those packages already installed, so the first scheduled upgrade has almost nothing to do. Newly released patches still install automatically afterward, in small amounts.

| | Disable timers (earlier idea) | Patch at build, keep timers (new) |
|---|---|---|
| Automatic patching stays | no | yes |
| Boot-time CPU spike | gone | probably much smaller |
| Compliance question | needed | not needed |
| Cost | none | a few minutes longer builds |

I haven't tested this on your image. The first build after the change will show the result in the CPU graph.

**Add these two lines to the main shell provisioner,** in both `backend.pkr.hcl` and `frontend.pkr.hcl`, as the first lines of the `inline` list, before the Node.js line:
```
      "sudo apt-get update",
      "sudo DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive apt-get -y upgrade",
```
Risk: if an upgrade restarts the SSH service during the build, Packer can lose its connection and fail the build. The earlier logs show restarts being deferred, so I expect it to work. A failed build changes nothing live, and I'd need the log tail.

**Effects:** no downtime, a normal rolling update, and no new cost. The image becomes patched as of its build date, which is also what your audit would see.

**Rollback:** revert the commit and push.

**Verify after the next deploy.** The new server boots from the new image, and about 45 minutes later its upgrade timer fires. Check:
```bash
sudo tail -n 5 /var/log/unattended-upgrades/unattended-upgrades.log | cut -c1-170
```
It should say there's nothing to upgrade or only a few packages, and the group should stay at 1 server. Wait until both groups are at 1 before pushing.

